WEBVTT

00:00:00.156 --> 00:00:03.527
Use this guide when selecting advanced SQL tools.

00:00:03.527 --> 00:00:07.332
Named stages suggest CTEs. Hierarchies suggest recursive CTEs.

00:00:07.332 --> 00:00:10.087
Detail plus group context suggests windows.

00:00:10.087 --> 00:00:12.282
Top N requires a ranking rule.

00:00:12.282 --> 00:00:14.747
Previous and next suggest LAG and LEAD.

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:19.347
Running and moving calculations require ordered windows and meaningful frames.

00:00:19.347 --> 00:00:25.482
And if you truly want one row per group, GROUP BY may still be the simplest correct choice.

00:00:25.482 --> 00:00:29.967
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:29.967 --> 00:00:35.282
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:37.022
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:37.022 --> 00:00:42.312
In a real setting such as a sales team calculating running revenue and top products by region,

00:00:42.312 --> 00:00:47.552
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:47.552 --> 00:00:51.612
before discussing syntax.

00:00:51.612 --> 00:00:56.471
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
